 47FE c  2014Po05: {+48}Ni isotope produced in fragmentation of natural Ni      
 47FE2c   target with a {+58}Ni beam with E({+58}Ni)=160 MeV/nucleon at         
 47FE3c  NSCL at MSU. Fragments separated                                       
 47FE4c  with the A1900 fragment separator in the achromatic setting and        
 47FE5c  identified using time-of-flight and energy-loss techniques using a     
 47FE6c  plastic scintillator located at the focal plane of the A1900 and a Si  
 47FE7c  detector placed at the end of the beam line. The optical time          
 47FE8c  projection chamber (OTPC) was used to detect fragments and the decay   
 47FE9c  of heavy particles such as protons or |a particles. Light from the     
 47FEac  ionization by the stopping ion or emitted charged particle was         
 47FEbc  registered with a digital camera (CCD) and a photomulitplier tube      
 47FEcc  (PMT).                                                                 
 47FEdc  Nine events corresponding to {+48}Ni were registered. Two were not     
 47FEec  stopped in the active volume of the OPTC, thus no decay information    
 47FEfc  could be obtained. Two were interpreted as |bp decay and four          
 47FEgc  represented 2p decay. For two of the 2p decay events, the subsequent   
 47FEhc  decay of {+46}Fe by |b-delayed proton emission was also recorded. For  
 47FEic  the last event, no decay signature was observed, probably indicating   
 47FEjc  that the decay occurred within 100 |ms following implantation when     
 47FEkc  the OTPC has low sensitivity.                                          
 47FElc  Total proton branching ratio based on the observed 4:2 ratio between   
 47FEmc  2p and |bp decay events.                                               
 47FEnc  Production cross section for {+48}Ni determined to be 150 fb {I50}.    
 47FE c  2011Po09: {+48}Ni produced at the NSCL                                 
 47FE2c  through fragmentation of a {+58}Ni beam, E=160 MeV/nucleon, impinging  
 47FE3c  on a 580 mg/cm{+2} thick natural nickel target. Products were          
 47FE4c  selected by the A1900 fragment separator and identified by time of     
 47FE5c  flight and energy loss information then slowed in an aluminum foil and 
 47FE6c  stopped in the active volume of the optical time-projection chamber    
 47FE7c  (OTPC) which was filled with a mixture of He, Ar, and N gases and used 
 47FE8c  to track charged particles. Measured |DE, ToF. Deduced T{-1/2}.        
 47FE9c  Results also reported in 2012Po03                                      
